  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Using a human centered-design to improve existing Child Labour Monitoring and Remediation System (CLMRS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, ECOM partnered with The Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO) and its Fund Against Child Labour (FBK) consultancy Propellerfish on a project to understand how to protect children in cocoa communities from hazardous activities.   The project is co-funded by FBK and the Dutch Ministry of Foreign Affairs. Phase A of the project involved investigating the causes of child labour by developing a human-centred design approach to refine our CLMRS based on conversations with cocoa-growing communities.  In 2024, the project entered Phase B, focusing on expanding access to sustainable and innovative technologies such as labour-saving tools, while creating job opportunities for young people. The project supported the improvement of school management systems and promoted women&#x2019;s entrepreneurship and access to microfinance. It also strengthened company due diligence processes and developed action plans to address child labour beyond the funding period. The programme supported productivity and youth engagement through subsidised services such as pruning and weeding, combined with the establishment of community-based labour groups.Implementation took place in 2024&#x2013;2025 across 10 communities in four cooperatives in C&#xf4;te d&#x2019;Ivoire. In total, 509 farmers expressed interest and 437 enrolled, with 18 labour groups established, trained, and equipped. A co-financing model, where farmers contributed 20% of costs, supported both uptake and long-term ownership. School governance and community-led education systems were also improved. This included improving needs assessments in high-risk communities, with six schools assessed to date, alongside the establishment of community child protection committees. The development of school management committees (COGES), school canteen systems, and education support initiatives such as reading clubs is planned for 2026.The project also improved women&#x2019;s economic empowerment through the establishment and support of Village Savings and Loan Associations (VSLAs). These groups promote financial inclusion, gender equality, and income-generating activities. In total, 29 VSLAs were supported, including nine newly created groups, reaching around 650 members.Most groups have already received training on financial literacy and entrepreneurship, with further capacity-building and links to microfinance institutions planned. Due diligence systems were integrated, which included developing a baseline and a mixed-methods Monitoring, Evaluation, and Learning (MEL) framework in partnership with KIT to assess impacts on child labour, income, and child wellbeing.  It also included strengthening supplier due diligence through risk-based segmentation and embedding due diligence principles into procurement decisions. Overall, the results support a more structured, evidence-driven approach to child labour prevention. ECOM and the project partners aim to develop a multi-year plan to expand successful interventions beyond the project period by integrating them into existing customer sustainability programmes and securing co-financing. This plan will be developed based on validated results and will guide expansion to additional cooperatives and communities.</narrative>
  </description>
